Composite and rig the **Aegis**, a colossal multi-gun walking fortress that strides on
legs, aims a dominant main cannon forward, works a side-mounted turret on each flank,
and sweeps a radar vane — as a 120x110x150 model **meshed with Dual Contouring** using
only the `dc-anim` tool. You build it not by painting cells but by **compositing a
signed-distance field** — adding and subtracting primitives — and you **author its
walk and weapon animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Aegis is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the fortress into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a walking, firing fortress
needs.

## How compositing and rigging works

The only way to build a part and edit the rig is the `dc-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation at a
time**, and the geometry it **emits** — one per-part `.glb` (binary glTF) mesh —
together with the rig you define are the **authoritative output that is scored**, not
any file you write yourself. Anything produced another way (writing a mesh directly,
editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the tool-emitted meshes and
the rig count.

Unlike painting discrete voxels, `dc-anim` builds each part's geometry as a
**continuous signed-distance field**: you **add** primitives (spheres, boxes,
ellipsoids, cylinders — each a center/extent and an opaque `#rrggbb` color) to lay
down material and **subtract** them to carve it away, optionally with a soft `--blend`
radius that fuses primitives smoothly (a `--blend` of `0`, the default, is a hard
union with a genuine crease). The binary then extracts the field's surface into a
triangle mesh with Dual Contouring, which reconstructs sharp edges and corners
faithfully instead of rounding them, and exposes a `--sharp` / `--smooth` tag per
primitive that holds or rounds its edges.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every field operation
targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it;
each part is a **separate field** composited in the shared volume's coordinates. The
field starts **empty**. Colors are **opaque** `#rrggbb` — there is no transparency.
Each call **only records the operation to that part's log** — it meshes and renders
nothing. When you want to extract the geometry and (re)draw the previews and assembled
scene, run `dc-anim render`: plain `dc-anim render` re-emits **every** part's `.glb`
mesh and preview and draws the assembled `scene/` views, `dc-anim render --component
<part>` renders just one part, and `dc-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at that instant of an animation so you can check a walk cycle.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
dc-anim define-part --name chassis
dc-anim add-box --part chassis --cx 60 --cy 60 --cz 75 --sx 84 --sy 40 --sz 120 --color "#c69a4b" --sharp
dc-anim add-sphere --part main_turret --cx 60 --cy 86 --cz 80 --r 16 --blend 3 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`dc-anim --help` to list every field operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and their keyframes),
and `dc-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is
the authoritative contract. **Run `dc-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to
see that part's progress, **and read the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
fortress composed from all your parts, drawn whenever you run `dc-anim render`. The scene
views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together and that the legs reach the
ground. Because each part is composited in the shared volume's coordinates, position
each part where it belongs on the finished fortress; the scene simply composes them
there.

