## What to deliver

Build and rig the fortress up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a heavily armored citadel raised on legs, carrying
a big main turret and forward cannon, a side-mounted turret out on each flank, and a
sweeping radar vane — all as a crisp, high-fidelity Dual Contouring surface with sharp
edges and corners preserved. **Author the three required animations** as F-curves with
the `dc-anim` animation subcommands: `march` (a walk whose feet plant flat and still,
then lift and swing — with weight, not a weightless slide), `bombardment` (the guns
working while the fortress stands), and the self-playing `radar_spin`. The animations
are the fixed contract — produce all three, by those names; the parts and joints that
realize them are yours. **Before you finish, run `dc-anim render` (no options)** so
every part's surface is extracted and emitted to its `.glb` — an unrendered part leaves
no geometry and is scored as empty (`--time`/`--animation` previews an animation,
`--component` renders one part). When you are satisfied, stop: the emitted per-part
meshes and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
